Kubernetes + Helm + Terraform – MongoDB Demo Platform

✅ Objective

This project demonstrates a production-style Kubernetes platform built using:

Terraform for AWS infrastructure

Helm for MongoDB and application deployment

EKS (Kubernetes) as the runtime

A tiny demo application that reads/writes data to MongoDB

The focus of this project is on:

Infrastructure as Code (IaC)

Helm chart authoring

Secure configuration using Secrets

Production-ready defaults

Clear, repeatable verification steps

🧱 Architecture Overview

Terraform provisions:

VPC with public & private subnets

Internet Gateway, NAT Gateway

EKS Cluster

Managed Node Group

IAM roles and policies

On Kubernetes:

MongoDB deployed using Bitnami Helm chart

Demo application deployed using custom Helm chart

Persistent storage via EBS CSI Driver

Secrets for secure credentials

Health probes and resource limits enabled

Flow:

User → orders-app (Service) → MongoDB (Service) → EBS PVC

⚙️ Tech Stack
Layer	Tool
Cloud	AWS
IaC	Terraform
Container Runtime	Docker
Orchestration	Kubernetes (EKS)
Package Manager	Helm
Database	MongoDB Community
Application	Python + FastAPI
Registry	AWS ECR
🏗️ Infrastructure Provisioning (Terraform)
Steps
cd infra
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply


Terraform creates:

VPC + networking

EKS cluster

Managed node group

Cluster Access

Since this was executed from an EC2 instance using Session Manager, cluster access was granted using:

EKS Access Entries with AmazonEKSClusterAdminPolicy

This replaces the older aws-auth ConfigMap and is the modern AWS-recommended approach.

💾 MongoDB Deployment (Helm)
Helm Chart Used

Bitnami MongoDB Helm chart

Reason: Well-maintained, supports auth, PVC, probes, and production features

Values File: values.dev.yaml

Key features:

Authentication enabled

Persistent storage with gp2 StorageClass

ClusterIP Service

Resource requests and limits defined

Deployment Command
helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
helm repo update

helm upgrade --install mongo bitnami/mongodb \
  -n mongo \
  -f values.dev.yaml

Verification
kubectl get pods -n mongo
kubectl get pvc -n mongo
kubectl get svc -n mongo


✅ PVC → Bound
✅ Pod → Running

🧪 Demo Application (Custom Helm Chart)
Application Features

GET /healthz → Mongo ping health check

POST /orders → Inserts {orderId, ts} into Mongo

GET /orders/count → Returns document count

Structured single-line logs

Environment-based configuration

Environment Variables
Variable	Source
MONGO_URI	Kubernetes Secret
MONGO_DB	Helm values
MONGO_COLLECTION	Helm values
🐳 Docker & ECR
Build Image
docker build -t orders-app:v1 .

Push to ECR
aws ecr get-login-password --region us-east-1 \
  | sudo docker login --username AWS --password-stdin <ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com

sudo docker tag orders-app:v1 <ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/orders-app:v1
sudo docker push <ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/orders-app:v1

🚀 App Deployment (Helm)
cd helm
helm upgrade --install orders-app ./orders-app -n mongo


Verification:

kubectl get pods -n mongo
kubectl get svc -n mongo

✅ Functional Testing (Acceptance Criteria)
    Port Forward
    kubectl port-forward svc/orders-app -n mongo 8080:80

    Test Endpoints
    curl http://localhost:8080/healthz

    curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/orders \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{"orderId":"order-123"}'

    curl http://localhost:8080/orders/count

    Logs
    kubectl logs deploy/orders-app -n mongo


    Example:

    method=POST path=/orders status=200 latency_ms=12
    method=GET path=/orders/count status=200 latency_ms=4


✅ All acceptance criteria satisfied.

🛠️ Major Issues Faced & How They Were Fixed
1️⃣ kubectl get nodes → Unauthorized

 Cause: No cluster access mapped to EC2 IAM role
 Fix: Used EKS Access Entry + AmazonEKSClusterAdminPolicy

2️⃣ MongoDB Pod Stuck in Pending

 Cause:
 EBS CSI Driver not installed

 Fix:
 Installed Amazon EBS CSI Driver
 Installed EKS Pod Identity Agent
 Associated AmazonEBSCSIDriverPolicy to CSI Controller ServiceAccount
 Reinstalled MongoDB

3️⃣ EBS CSI Controller CrashLoopBackOff

 Cause: Missing IAM role for controller
 Fix: Created IAM role using Pod Identity and reattached policy

4️⃣ Helm Install Failing due to values.yaml YAML Errors

 Cause: Broken indentation
 Fix: Replaced entire values.yaml with a clean validated version

5️⃣ App Pod in ImagePullBackOff

 Cause: Image tag v1 did not exist in ECR
 Fix: Properly built, tagged, logged into ECR, and pushed image

6️⃣ no basic auth credentials During Docker Push

 Cause: ECR login was done as normal user but push was with sudo
 Fix: Logged into ECR again using:
 aws ecr get-login-password | sudo docker login ...

7️⃣ Python Virtual Environment Permission Issues

 Cause: Project folder created using sudo
 Fix:
 sudo chown -R ssm-user:ssm-user ~/devops-daily-practices
 Recreated venv and reinstalled dependencies successfully.

🔐 Security Highlights

 No secrets hardcoded in Git
 MongoDB password stored in Kubernetes Secret
 IAM access managed using EKS Access Entries
 EBS access secured using Pod Identity
 Application runs using least-privilege model

📈 Optional Tier-B Extensions (Not Implemented Yet)

 NetworkPolicy between app and MongoDB
 HPA for autoscaling based on CPU
 Metrics scraping using Prometheus
 Ingress exposure with TLS

🏁 Conclusion

 This project demonstrates:
 Real-world Kubernetes troubleshooting
 Secure AWS + EKS integration
 Production-style Helm deployments
 End-to-end app + database connectivity
